
===== story:onepager (37s, 4517 chars)
Zander Demo is a former Operations Director who grew a HVAC division from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years at Harlow & Pike Building Services, fixing the same problems his clients face today. He now works with MDs and owners of M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting firms who are winning work but not banking what they expected.

- **£4.2m to £11.8m** HVAC division revenue grown at Harlow & Pike in five years, with margin held throughout
- **8** Maximum clients carried at once, so each gets proper time and attention
- **£1,950/month** Minimum engagement fee, covering weekly numbers rhythm, job reviews and direct access

### Who I help

My core client is the MD or owner of an M&E, HVAC or electrical contracting firm turning over between £2m and £12m, typically with 12 to 40 people on the payroll. They have been in the business ten years or more, often started on the tools or came up through estimating, and they are winning more work than ever while taking less home than they expected. The order book is solid, the lads are busy, and the final account keeps coming back short. They cannot fully explain the gap, and they know the same thing is probably happening on the next scheme.

### The problems I fix

- Margin bleeding out on schemes with no clear explanation: the Wakefield job runs twelve weeks, the lads are flat out, the applications go in on time, and the final account comes back £40k to £80k short with nobody in the business able to tell the MD where it went.
- Retentions sitting unpaid for months with nobody chasing them: money earned on jobs closed a year or more ago, sitting idle, some of it quietly written off because the main contractor has moved on.
- MD stuck in the operational middle: every site call, every variation, every decision still comes through to the owner because the contracts manager and site team do not have the rhythm or the authority to handle it themselves.
- Management accounts arriving six weeks late describing what already happened, while live schemes run on gut feel with no weekly visibility on whether a job is making or losing money while there is still time to act.

### How I work

1. **Weeks one to four: get into the operation.** I sit in on job reviews and pricing discussions, map the retentions position, and find where the margin is bleeding. This is hands-on from day one. There is no warm-up period and no months spent explaining how the industry works.
2. **Month two onwards: build the weekly rhythm.** Two 90-minute sessions a month, a standing weekly check on the numbers across live schemes, and direct access when something on a job needs a call. The rhythm is built around what is happening now, not last month's management accounts.
3. **Six months in: visible, traceable results.** Margin recovered on live jobs, retentions chased and banked, pricing sharpened before the next tender goes in, and the MD out of the operational middle. The numbers are tracked from the start so the improvement is not theoretical.

### Results

- Grew the HVAC division at Harlow & Pike Building Services from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years by shifting the model from one-off installs to planned maintenance contracts and holding margin through disciplined job pricing and cost control.
- Fifteen years inside building services, from Project Engineer at Northern M&E through Contracts Manager to Operations Director, with direct experience of retentions, job costing, variation management and the day-to-day pressure of running a live contracting operation.

### Working together

If you work with MDs or owners of M&E, HVAC or electrical contracting firms, the clients most likely to benefit from an introduction are the ones winning work but quietly frustrated by where the profit goes. They may have mentioned final accounts coming back short, retentions sitting unpaid, or the fact that they cannot take a week off without the phone going. They are not looking for coaching in the abstract. They want someone who already knows the industry, will get into the numbers quickly, and will stay in them. That is what I do. I carry a maximum of eight clients, so introductions go to the right people, not a waiting list. The fee starts at £1,950 a month. If the client you have in mind fits that picture, I am happy to have a straight conversation.

### Working together

- **LinkedIn:** linkedin.com/in/zander-demo
- **Website:** Profit Coach page under Zander Demo
- **Location:** Leeds, West Yorkshire
- **Prospect calls:** Mondays and Thursdays from 1pm

===== voice:sound (24s, 2679 chars)
Zander talks like someone who has stood on a live site and got the phone call at 7am. He is direct, unpretentious, and grounded in the actual language of contracting.

- **Tone:** Straight-talking, grounded, dry, unhurried, credible
- **Pace:** Measured. He makes his point and stops. No padding.
- **Formality:** Low. First names from the off. No corporate register.
- **Humour:** Dry and sparing. Occasionally self-aware. Never performed.
- **Regional flavour:** West Yorkshire. 'Aye', 'nowt', 'lads'. Comfortable with it, not overdone.

### Sounds like

- 'It's usually the same three things if I'm honest. Margin, the MD stuck in the middle of everything, and retention cash nobody's chasing.'
- 'I've got nowt to offer a start-up. My experience is fixing and scaling what's already there.'
- 'Eight clients is the right number. Enough to build something meaningful, not so many I'm giving people half my attention.'

### Does not sound like

- 'I empower contractors to unlock their true profit potential and elevate their operational performance.'
- 'Let's unpack the key challenges facing your business in today's competitive landscape.'
- 'It's not about working harder. It's about working smarter and leveraging the right systems.'

### Words and phrases to use

- Lads, engineers, subbies, site
- Schemes, jobs, the Wakefield job
- Retentions, holdbacks, money sat out there
- Final account, the FA
- Variations, vars, extras
- Applications, apps
- Job costing, margin, pricing a job
- Weekly numbers rhythm
- Get into the numbers
- Main contractor, the MC
- Aye, nowt, properly
- 'That counts for a lot'
- 'I can talk to it straight'

### Words to avoid

- Leverage (as a verb)
- Empower, elevate, unlock
- Robust, seamless, crucial
- Dive deep, unpack, navigate
- Journey, ecosystem, bandwidth
- In today's world
- Coaching cliches: accountability partner, transformational, holistic
- Any phrase that sounds like it came from a LinkedIn carousel

### A message in your voice

**Example**

```text
Hi Mike, I work with MDs of M&E and HVAC firms who are winning good work but taking less home than they expected, and can't fully explain why. I spent fifteen years in building services, finishing up as Operations Director at Harlow & Pike, where we had exactly that problem. Fixed it by getting proper visibility on live job costs before the final account landed. If that sounds familiar, I'm happy to have a straight conversation about what's happening on your schemes. No pitch, just a useful half hour. Worth a chat?
```

_Use on a first LinkedIn message or email to an MD in M&E, HVAC or electrical contracting. Keep it this length. Do not add a list of services._

===== market:options (30s, 3814 chars)
Zander's strongest market is HVAC and M&E contracting firms, where his Harlow & Pike proof is direct, the pain is acute, and the buyer already speaks his language.

| Option | Value | Pain | Growing | Easy to find | Purchasing power |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| HVAC and M&E contracting firms (£1m–£15m) | 5 | 5 | 4 | 4 | 4 |
| Planned maintenance and FM contractors (£2m–£20m) | 4 | 4 | 4 | 3 | 4 |
| Electrical contracting businesses (£1m–£10m) | 3 | 4 | 4 | 3 | 3 |

### Option 1: HVAC and M&E contracting firms

This is where Zander's proof is sharpest and most direct. He grew the HVAC division at Harlow & Pike from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years by fixing margin bleed, tightening job pricing, and shifting to planned maintenance contracts. Every MD in an M&E or HVAC firm knows that story and knows what it cost to learn it. The three problems Zander solves - margin disappearing on jobs with no explanation, retention cash sitting idle, and the MD fielding every site call - are not occasional headaches in this sector. They are the default condition of a growing contracting business. The buyer here has typically tried a spreadsheet, promoted a contracts manager, and brought in an accountant, and none of it fixed the core problem. Zander's operational background removes the single biggest objection in this market: that he will spend the first three months getting the client to explain what a retention is.

### Option 2: Planned maintenance and FM contractors

Facilities management and planned maintenance contractors share the same operational DNA as HVAC and M&E firms, and Zander understands the model from the inside. The shift to planned maintenance contracts was the mechanism behind the Harlow & Pike growth, so he can speak to the commercial logic of that model with authority. Owner dependency is particularly acute in this sector: the MD is often the relationship with the client, the sign-off on every callout, and the person the engineers ring when something is not on the job sheet. The purchasing power is solid and the businesses are findable through FM associations and procurement networks. The reason this scores below Option 1 is that Zander's proof is applied rather than direct - he led the shift into this model, but his operational home was HVAC. That matters when the buyer is a sceptical MD who has been burned by generalists before.

### Option 3: Electrical contracting businesses

Electrical contractors face the same cash flow and margin problems as M&E firms, and Zander's sector knowledge is credible here. The pain is real: retentions sitting unpaid, applications going in on time and the money still not moving, jobs that looked profitable at tender coming back short at the final account. The gap is proof. There is no specific electrical contractor result to point to, which matters when the buyer's first question is likely to be whether Zander has fixed this in a business like theirs. Until a client result exists in this sub-sector, the outreach pitch requires more education and the conversion will be slower. Worth revisiting once a client from electrical contracting is on the books.

### Recommended core client

> My core client is the MD or owner of an HVAC or M&E contracting firm with 10 to 50 employees doing £1M to £20M revenue.
>
> Zander Demo

> **If Options 1 and 2 feel equally close on your Decision Call:** Ask yourself one question: which MD would feel the most relief the moment you explained how you grew the Harlow & Pike HVAC division? That is your market. HVAC and M&E owners will feel it immediately because the job is identical. FM owners will get it, but you will need a sentence or two more. Start where the proof lands fastest. You can extend into FM once you have two or three client results to point to in that space.

===== market:avatar (65s, 8621 chars)
Margin-Blind Mike: MD of a 25-person M&E contracting firm in West Yorkshire, winning more work than ever and taking less home than he did three years ago.

- **Name:** Mike
- **Age:** 42 to 54
- **Location:** West Yorkshire / East Midlands
- **Business:** M&E or HVAC contracting firm, typically founder-led or second-generation
- **Revenue:** £2m to £12m
- **Team:** 12 to 40 people: engineers, supervisors, a contracts manager, one person in the office doing everything else
- **Role:** MD and owner. Also de facto site manager, pricing director, and first call for every problem
- **Time in the business:** Typically 10 to 25 years. Started as an engineer or estimator, built the firm up himself or took it over from his father

### A day in the life

It is 6.47am and Mike is already in the van, phone wedged against the steering wheel. Dave, his contracts manager, has rung twice before eight because the MC on the Wakefield job has moved the programme again and nobody on site knows whether to pull the plumbers off or keep them running. Mike knows the answer immediately, but that is the problem: he always knows the answer, which means nobody else ever has to find one. He gets to the office at half eight, meaning to spend the morning on a tender that needs to go in by Thursday. By eleven he has answered fourteen messages, approved two material orders he told Dave to approve last month, and taken a call from a main contractor chasing a variation that should have been agreed six weeks ago. The tender is still on page one. At lunchtime he pulls up the job cost sheet for the Wakefield scheme on his laptop. The numbers look roughly right but he cannot tell whether roughly right means they are making the margin they priced, or losing it slowly in a way that will only become obvious when the final account lands in four months. He stares at it for a moment, then closes the lid. He has got a site visit at two and another call at four. He will look at it properly tomorrow. He will not look at it properly tomorrow. On the drive home he does the maths in his head: turnover is up, the lads are flat out, and yet he is fairly sure he will take less home this year than the year before last. He cannot explain why. That is what bothers him most.

### In his words

> The revenue's there. The work's there. The lads are busy. So why am I sat here working out if I can justify a week off in September without the whole thing falling over? We had a good year on paper, everyone tells me that, my accountant tells me that, but I'll probably take less home than two years ago and I genuinely cannot tell you where it went. The Wakefield job looked solid when we priced it. I've been through the final account twice and I can still not properly explain the gap. It's not like anyone's robbing us. The money's just... not there. And I keep thinking if I just got a bit more on top of it, got the right spreadsheet or sat down with the right person, I'd be able to see it. But I've got no time to do that because I'm still the one Dave rings when the MC changes the programme. I told him six months ago he needs to handle that himself. He agrees every time I say it and then the phone goes at quarter to seven and it's him. I don't blame him. I've never properly shown him what to do instead. I just assumed he'd... figure it out. I've got retention money sat out there from a job we finished eighteen months ago. Janet mentioned it last week. I said I'd chase it. I haven't. I don't even know the exact figure off the top of my head and I should, it's my money. I'm not an idiot. I know what the problems are. I just don't know how to fix them without making it a full-time project on top of a full-time job. Maybe I just need to get better at delegating. Maybe I need a better system. Maybe it's me. I don't really know.
>
> Mike, MD, M&E contracting firm, West Yorkshire

### What they want

- Know at week four whether a live scheme is making or losing margin, with enough time to do something about it before the final account tells him it is too late.
- Stop being the first call for site decisions. Take a week off and come back to a business that managed without him, not a backlog that only he can clear.
- Get the retention money moving. Every holdback chased on a set schedule, by someone accountable for it, so the cash the business has earned actually lands in the account.
- Understand which jobs, which clients, and which contract types actually make money, so the next tender goes in right.
- Build something he could step back from one day, or sell for a number that reflects twenty years of work, rather than a business that only runs because he is in the middle of it.

### What they fear

- That the margin bleed is not a bad patch. That it is structural, baked into the way the business prices and runs jobs, and has been going on for years without him seeing it.
- That if Ste or Dave handed their notice in tomorrow, he would be in serious trouble, because too much of what makes the operation run is in their heads and not written down anywhere.
- That he has spent twenty years building something that only works because he is inside it, and when he finally wants to step back there will be nothing worth selling.
- That the business is growing in a way he cannot control, and the next big scheme will tip it into a crisis he cannot manage from the middle.

### What they have tried

- Built his own job cost spreadsheet. Uses it on some jobs, not others. It is never quite up to date and he is not confident the numbers are telling him anything useful until it is too late to act.
- Promoted a senior engineer to contracts manager and told him to handle site decisions. The calls still come through because nobody was clear on what Dave was actually supposed to own.
- Brought in an accountant for quarterly management accounts. Gets them six weeks after the period ends, by which point the job that was bleeding margin has already finished.
- Had a consultant in two years ago. Got a report with twenty-three recommendations. Implemented three of them. The margin problem is still there.

### What they suspect

- That most coaches have never been near a live site and will spend the first three months getting him to explain what a retention is or how an application works.
- That they will produce a framework that looks good in a slide deck and does not survive contact with a main contractor who has just changed the programme.
- That the fee will be real and the results will be vague, and he will be the one doing most of the work while they ask him how it makes him feel.
- That his business genuinely is different enough that something built for a generic SME will not apply to the way contracting actually works.

### What finally makes them act

- A final account comes back £50k to £80k short on a job that looked solid at tender and nobody in the business can explain the gap, including Mike.
- A retention from a job closed eighteen months ago gets quietly written off because the main contractor has moved on and nobody was chasing it consistently.
- Mike sits down and works out what he actually took home last year against the hours he put in. The number shocks him.
- A holiday or a family commitment gets cancelled because a site issue needs him personally, and someone close to him says something he cannot unhear.
- A trusted site supervisor or contracts manager hands in his notice and Mike realises how much of what makes the business run is about to walk out the door with him.

### How they talk

| Thing | What they call it |
| --- | --- |
| Clients / end users | Clients, end users, the client, the building owner |
| Main contractors | The MC, main contractor, the principal |
| Direct staff (engineers, plumbers, electricians) | The lads, the engineers, the guys on the tools |
| Subcontractors | Subbies |
| Projects | Jobs, schemes, the Wakefield job, the contract |
| Retention money | Retentions, holdbacks, money sat out there |
| Final account | The FA, the final account |
| Variation orders / extras | Vars, variations, extras, change orders |
| Payment applications | Apps, applications |
| Profit on a job | Margin, what we made on it, what it came back at |

### Ready to act

> I need to know where the margin's going before the final account tells me. And I need to stop being the person the business can't run without. I'm not looking for a consultant who'll write me a report. I want someone who's actually been in this industry, who can get into the numbers with me and help me sort it properly.
>
> Mike, MD, M&E contracting firm, West Yorkshire

