### Nurture rhythm

- **Week 2:** Send Profit Maximiser. First value message. Personalise the opening line to something specific from your earlier conversation - a job they mentioned, a problem they named. Send the tool with no strings. Follow up three to four days later with the five-step expense review as promised.
- **Week 5:** Send Cashflow Forecaster. Second value message. Lead with retentions or cash flow if either came up before. If not, use the rolling 13-week framing - it applies to almost every contractor at this size. Keep the opening line specific to them.
- **Week 8:** Send Time Value Tracker. Third value message. This one works best when the prospect has mentioned being stretched, not taking holidays, or being the bottleneck. Frame it around what they said, not around the tool itself.
- **Day 90:** Check-in: one short question. Send a brief, direct message. Something like: 'It's been a couple of months since we last spoke. Is the margin visibility question still one you're looking at, or has something shifted?' No tool, no pitch. One question. If they re-engage, move to the BOSS Scorecard or a prospect call. If not, keep them on a light monthly touch - a relevant post, a one-liner when something industry-relevant happens.

===== offer:direction (42s, 5855 chars)
Zander Demo helps MDs of M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting firms find where the margin is going and get out of the operational middle, without another consultant report that gathers dust, using a weekly numbers rhythm built around live job costs and clear accountability.

### The gap

| Today | What they want |
| --- | --- |
| Margin priced at 12% banking at 4% with no clear explanation until the final account lands | Know by week four whether a live scheme is making or losing money, with time to act on the job that is still running |
| Revenue growing, hours increasing, take-home shrinking - and the MD cannot explain the gap | Understand which jobs, clients and contract types actually make money, so the next tender goes in right |
| MD fielding every site call, approving every order, still the first number Dave rings at quarter to seven | Contracts manager and site team making their own calls; MD steps in only when it genuinely needs him |
| Retention money sitting out there for months, nobody accountable for chasing it, some quietly written off | Every holdback chased on a set schedule by one person, with the cash the business has already earned landing in the account |

### Why they will buy

- Know-how: Mike has tried spreadsheets, promoted a senior engineer, brought in a consultant, and got quarterly accounts six weeks too late. None of it fixed the margin problem. He needs someone who already understands retentions, final accounts, variation orders and MC programme changes, not someone he has to educate first.
- Speed: Every scheme that runs without live job cost visibility is another gap at the final account. Waiting another quarter, another job, another year costs real money. The weekly rhythm gives Mike sight of problems while there is still time to do something about them.
- Ease: Mike does not have time for another project on top of a full-time job. He needs someone to bring the structure and sit in the numbers alongside him, not hand him a framework and wish him luck.
- Fit: Generic SME coaching does not survive contact with a main contractor who has just moved the programme. Mike already suspects that. Zander's background removes the objection before Mike has to raise it.

### Key messages

1. The revenue is there. The lads are flat out. The final account tells a different story. Margin bleed in contracting is almost always a job costing and pricing problem, and it compounds across every scheme until someone gets into the live numbers and finds where it is happening.
2. Being the person Dave rings is not a management problem, it is a structural one. Until the contracts manager has clear accountability and a rhythm to work to, the calls will keep coming through to the MD, whatever he tells them.
3. Retention money is earned. It is sitting out there with a name on it. Without one person accountable for chasing every holdback on a fixed schedule, some of it will get written off quietly, and the cash flow pressure will force decisions from the wrong position.
4. A six-week-old management account cannot tell you whether the Wakefield job is making or losing margin this week. By the time the final account lands, the damage is done and the only lesson available is an expensive one.
5. A business that only runs because the MD is inside it has no step-back, no sale value, and no end point. Twenty years of work should be worth more than a van and a list of contracts.

### The offer

- **Name:** The Contractor's Profit Rhythm
- **Promise:** Know where the margin is going before the final account tells you. Stop being the person the business cannot run without. Get the retention cash you have already earned moving on a proper schedule.
- **Who it is for:** MDs and owners of M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting firms turning over between £2m and £12m. Founder-led or family businesses where the MD is still in the operational middle and margin is disappearing without a clear explanation.
- **What is included:** Weeks one to six: Zander sits in on job reviews and pricing discussions, maps where margin is bleeding, and identifies the retentions nobody is chasing. Builds the weekly numbers rhythm around your live job costs. Diagnoses where owner dependency is structural rather than habitual. From month two onwards: two 90-minute sessions a month working the rhythm, reviewing live scheme numbers, holding accountability on retentions, and shifting decisions down to the contracts manager and site team. Hybrid delivery - some sessions on site where it adds context.
- **Format:** Weekly sessions for the first four to six weeks, then two 90-minute sessions a month. Tuesdays and Wednesdays, 08:00 to 14:00. Hybrid: remote and on site as needed.
- **Price options:** £1,950 a month, retained. Six months upfront available at a reduced rate. Maximum eight clients carried at once.

### Why you

- Led the HVAC division at Harlow & Pike from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years, with margin held through the growth, by shifting the model from one-off installs to planned maintenance contracts and sharpening job pricing discipline.
- Progressed from Project Engineer at Northern M&E through Contracts Manager to Operations Director at Harlow & Pike. He has done the jobs his clients' people do, which means he can sit in a pricing meeting or a job review and understand exactly what the numbers are saying.
- Got there by fixing the same three problems his clients face today: margin bleeding out on schemes with no clear explanation, retentions sitting idle with nobody accountable, and the MD still fielding every call.
- No warm-up period. Zander gets into job reviews and pricing discussions from week one. Mike does not spend the first three months explaining what a retention is or how an application works.
- Eight clients maximum. Each one gets proper time, not a conveyor belt.

