_Use this at the end of the decision call, after you have established all three problems and the cost of them. Do not offer both options and then keep talking. Ask the question and wait._

### Proposal outline

1. **The situation.** Two or three sentences reflecting exactly what Mike told you on the call. His words, not yours. The Wakefield job, the margin gap he cannot explain, the phone at quarter to seven. This shows you were listening and makes everything that follows feel personal rather than templated.
2. **What it costs to leave it.** One number where possible. If a scheme came back £50k short, say so. If he worked out his hourly take-home and it shocked him, name that. The cost of inaction should be concrete, not vague. 'This costs you money every month you wait' is weak. 'The next FA will tell you the same story unless something changes between now and then' is specific.
3. **The Contractor's Profit Rhythm.** Name the offer. Describe what you do, not what you deliver. Weeks one to six: you sit in on job reviews and pricing discussions, map where margin is bleeding, identify the retentions nobody is chasing, and build the weekly numbers rhythm around live job costs. From month two: two 90-minute sessions a month, working the rhythm, reviewing live scheme numbers, holding accountability on retentions, and shifting decisions down to the contracts manager and site team. Hybrid where site context adds something.
4. **Why this works.** One paragraph, grounded in Harlow & Pike. Zander led the HVAC division from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years by fixing the same three problems. He is not applying a generic framework. He is building a rhythm around the way these businesses actually run. No warm-up period. He gets into the numbers from week one.
5. **The investment.** Present both options cleanly. Monthly: £1,950 a month, retained. Six months upfront: £10,500. Split option: 50% upfront, 50% at month three. State what is included (all sessions, hybrid delivery, access between sessions for live scheme questions). State what is not included (travel to site beyond a reasonable radius, if relevant).
6. **The next step.** One clear action. Not 'let me know if you have any questions.' Something like: 'If this looks right, reply to confirm which option works and I will send across the agreement and block your first session. I have Tuesday the [date] at 9am or Wednesday the [date] at 8am. Which suits you?' Make it easy to say yes and hard to drift.

### Raising your price

Raise your monthly rate by £500 every two to three clients you sign. You start at £1,950. At client three or four, move to £2,450. At client five or six, move to £2,950. New clients come in at the new rate. Existing clients stay where they are until you review. You do not need to announce the increase or justify it. You just quote the new number. If someone pushes back, the honest answer is that your capacity is limited to eight clients and the rate reflects that. At eight clients your monthly revenue sits between £15,600 and £23,600 depending on when you raised the rate, with a full book at the higher end bringing you close to £24,000 a month.

