The Margin Finder is a free 30-minute session where Zander gets into the numbers with an MD and shows them exactly where margin is most likely bleeding on their live schemes. The owner walks away with a clear picture of which job costing or pricing habit is costing them most, and a simple first step they can act on before the next final account lands.

- **Name:** The Margin Finder
- **Length:** 30 minutes
- **Who it is for:** MDs and owners of M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting firms, £2m to £12m turnover, where revenue is growing but take-home is shrinking and nobody can fully explain the gap
- **What they get:** A diagnostic conversation built around their live schemes. Zander identifies where margin is most likely leaking, whether retentions are being chased properly, and whether the MD is structurally stuck in the middle. They leave with two or three specific things to act on.
- **The promise:** No pitch, no fluff, no generic framework. A straight half hour with someone who has run a contracting operation and can talk to the numbers without needing a tour of how the industry works.

### The questions

| Area | Question | Red looks like | Green looks like |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Live job visibility | Can you tell me right now whether your three biggest live schemes are making or losing the margin you priced? | MD relies on gut feel or waits for the FA to know. Job cost sheet exists but is never current. | Live cost tracker updated weekly. MD can name the margin position on any scheme within 24 hours. |
| Job costing process | How do you know whether a job is running to cost at week four versus week twelve? | No interim review. Costs only reconciled at practical completion or when the FA lands. | Weekly or fortnightly cost review against the priced allowance. Variances flagged and acted on mid-scheme. |
| Pricing discipline | When you price a job at 12% margin, what happens between tender and final account that typically reduces what you bank? | MD can name the number but not the cause. Variations undercharged, prelims overspent, labour hours not tracked. | Clear picture of where margin erodes on which contract type. Pricing adjusted to account for it. |
| Retentions | How much retention money is currently outstanding, and who is accountable for chasing it on a fixed schedule? | MD has a rough number but no live list. Nobody owns the chase. Some holdbacks quietly written off. | Up-to-date retention schedule. One named person accountable. Regular chase cycle with dates and amounts. |
| Owner dependency | On a normal Tuesday, how many decisions come through to you that someone else in your team could have made? | MD is first call for site queries, material approvals and MC conversations. Contracts manager defers on everything. | Contracts manager handles site and MC decisions. MD steps in for commercial disputes and major programme changes only. |
| Management information | When do you typically find out a scheme is in trouble - during the job or at the final account? | Six-week-old management accounts. Margin problems visible only at the FA, when nothing can be done. | Weekly numbers rhythm. Live job costs reviewed against priced allowances while the scheme is still running. |
| Variations and extras | How do you track and value variations, and what percentage do you typically recover against what you raise? | Vars logged loosely or not at all. Recovery rate unknown. MC disputes variations from months ago because paperwork is missing. | Every variation raised, valued and submitted promptly. Recovery tracked. MD knows the gap at any point. |
| Team accountability | If your contracts manager handled everything on the Wakefield job without ringing you once, what would break? | MD cannot answer with confidence. Contracts manager does not have clear authority or a process to follow. | Contracts manager has defined decisions they own and a rhythm to work to. MD is a backstop, not the default. |
| Tender mix and profitability | Which contract types, clients or sectors actually make you money - and which look good at tender and come back short? | MD has a feeling but no data. All work treated the same at tender stage regardless of historical margin. | Clear picture of which jobs make margin and which do not. Tender decisions made with that data rather than against it. |
| Business dependency | If you were off for three weeks with no phone signal, what would the business do? | It would struggle. Key knowledge is in the MD's head or in one other person who would be a serious problem to lose. | It would run. Clear accountability, documented process, and a team that does not need the MD to answer every question. |

### Your opening questions

1. What is the biggest thing stopping your business from being where you want it right now? Whether that is margin, cash, the team, or something else entirely - what is the one thing that keeps coming back?
2. If we sat down again in twelve months and you were genuinely happy with where the business had got to, what would have changed? What would you be seeing in the numbers, and what would your week actually look like?
3. Why does sorting this matter to you personally, beyond the figures? Is it about what you take home, getting your time back, building something you could step away from one day, or something else?
4. What have you already tried? Spreadsheets, someone promoted to contracts manager, a consultant's report - what has happened so far, and what did and did not work about it?

