===== content:newsletter (35s, 4492 chars)
A short, practical newsletter for MDs of M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting firms who want to know where the margin is going before the final account tells them.

- **Name:** The Contractor's Profit Letter
- **Who it is for:** MDs and owners of M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting firms turning over £2m to £12m
- **How often:** Monthly
- **Format:** Email and LinkedIn newsletter - one idea, one story or example, one practical action, under 500 words
- **Call to action:** A free 45-minute value session with Zander to look at where margin is bleeding on live schemes

### The first issue

**Issue 1**

```text
Subject: The Wakefield job looked fine. It wasn't.

Here is a situation I see regularly in M&E and HVAC firms.

A job gets priced at twelve percent margin. It looks solid. The lads are on site, the MC is happy, applications are going in. Nobody raises a flag.

Then the final account lands. Margin came back at four percent. Sometimes less.

And when the MD sits down to work out what happened, there is no single explanation. A bit of labour overrun here. Some vars that never got agreed. A retention from eight months ago that nobody chased. Materials that crept up and nobody repriced.

The money did not disappear in one go. It bled out slowly across the life of the job, and by the time the FA made it visible, the scheme was finished and there was nothing left to do except absorb it.

The reason this keeps happening is not bad pricing at tender. Usually the tender is fine.

The reason is that most contracting businesses have no way of reading a live job. The management accounts come in six weeks after the period ends. The job cost spreadsheet is a month out of date. Nobody is looking at the numbers on a scheme that is still running with enough time to do something useful.

At Harlow & Pike, when I was running the HVAC division, this was exactly the problem. Jobs were coming in below margin and we could not see it until it was too late. What changed it was simple: we built a weekly numbers rhythm around live job costs. Not a new system. Not a new spreadsheet. A regular discipline of looking at what each scheme was actually costing while it was still running, and calling problems early enough to act on them.

Over five years, the division went from £4.2m to £11.8m, with margin held through the growth. The rhythm was most of it.

**One practical action for this month**

Pick your three biggest live schemes right now. For each one, write down two numbers: the margin you priced at tender, and your best honest estimate of where it is tracking today. If you cannot do that inside ten minutes, that is the gap worth fixing.

If you want to sit down and look at this properly, I offer a straight 45-minute conversation, no pitch, just a look at where the numbers are on your schemes and what a weekly rhythm might look like for your business. Book it here: [value session link]
```

_Send on the first Monday of each month. LinkedIn newsletter and email simultaneously. Keep the subject line specific to a job or situation the reader will recognise._

